%% Fact Book section for Topic 3 — included by content/FB/main.gsm under a "Topic 3" H1.

## T3 · The West: dates and milestones

::: table | The waves of Western feminism — timeline.
@widths 1.8,12.2
@font 9.5
Year | Event
1791 / 1792 | Olympe de Gouges, *Declaration of the Rights of Woman*; Wollstonecraft, *Vindication*
1839 | Custody of Infants Act (UK) after Caroline Norton's campaign
1840 | Stanton and Mott refused seats at the World Anti-Slavery Convention, London
1848 | Seneca Falls Convention (19–20 July); Declaration of Sentiments
1849 | Elizabeth Blackwell — first woman medical graduate in the USA
1851 | Sojourner Truth speaks at Akron, Ohio
1857 | Matrimonial Causes Act (UK)
1865 | Elizabeth Garrett Anderson qualifies as a doctor in Britain
1867 | J. S. Mill's amendment ("person" for "man")
1869 | NWSA and AWSA founded (USA); Wyoming Territory votes for women; Girton College
1870 / 1882 | Married Women's Property Acts (UK)
1886 | Contagious Diseases Acts repealed (Josephine Butler's campaign)
1890 | NAWSA formed by merger
1893 | New Zealand — women's vote (Kate Sheppard)
1897 | NUWSS (Millicent Fawcett)
1902 | Australia — federal vote (Aboriginal women excluded until 1962)
1903 | WSPU (Emmeline and Christabel Pankhurst)
1906 | Finland — vote and right to stand
1913 | Cat and Mouse Act; Emily Wilding Davison dies at the Derby
1917 | Alice Paul's National Woman's Party pickets the White House
1918 | Representation of the People Act (women over 30 with property)
1920 | 19th Amendment (USA)
1923 | Egyptian Feminist Union (Huda Shaarawi)
1928 | Equal Franchise Act (UK; vote at 21)
1934 / 1944 / 1963 | Turkey / France / Iran — women's vote
1949 | Beauvoir, *The Second Sex*
1960 | The pill approved (USA)
1961 | Kennedy's Commission on the Status of Women
1963 | Friedan, *The Feminine Mystique*; US Equal Pay Act
1964 | Civil Rights Act, Title VII
1965 | *Griswold v. Connecticut*; Voting Rights Act
1966 | NOW founded
1967 | UK Abortion Act
1968 | Miss America protest; Ford Dagenham strike; Lear, "The Second Feminist Wave"
1969 | Friedan's "lavender menace" remark; Hanisch writes "The Personal Is Political"
1970 | UK Equal Pay Act; Ruskin College conference; Millett; Firestone; Greer
1971 | Chiswick shelter (London); Manifesto of the 343 (France)
1972 | *Ms.* magazine; ERA passes Congress; Title IX
1973 | *Roe v. Wade* (22 January)
1975 | UK Sex Discrimination Act; Iceland women's day off (24 October); France legalises abortion
1980 | Vigdís Finnbogadóttir elected president of Iceland
1981 | hooks, *Ain't I a Woman*; Angela Davis, *Women, Race and Class*
1982 | ERA ratification deadline passes
1990 | Wolf, *The Beauty Myth*; Butler, *Gender Trouble*
1991 | Anita Hill hearings; Riot Grrrl; Faludi, *Backlash*; 16 Days of Activism begins
1992 | Rebecca Walker, "Becoming the Third Wave"
1994 | US Violence Against Women Act
1996 | *The Vagina Monologues*
2000 | Baumgardner and Richards, *Manifesta*
2006 | Tarana Burke's "Me Too"
2011 | SlutWalk (Toronto)
2012 | Everyday Sexism Project; Delhi gang-rape protests (December)
2013 | Cochrane, *All the Rebel Women*; India's Criminal Law (Amendment) Act
2015 | #NiUnaMenos (Argentina)
2017 | Women's March (21 January); #MeToo goes global (October)
2018 | Ireland repeals the Eighth Amendment (25 May)
2020 | Argentina legalises abortion (December)
2022 | *Dobbs v. Jackson* overturns *Roe*; "Woman, Life, Freedom" (Iran); Spain's consent law
:::

## T3 · The United Nations and women

::: table | The UN and women — timeline.
@widths 1.8,12.2
@font 9.5
Year | Event
1945 | UN Charter — "equal rights of men and women"
1946 | Commission on the Status of Women (21 June)
1948 | Universal Declaration of Human Rights (10 December — Human Rights Day)
1952 | Convention on the Political Rights of Women
1967 | Declaration on the Elimination of Discrimination against Women
1975 | International Women's Year; First World Conference, Mexico City; World Plan of Action
1976–1985 | UN Decade for Women — "Equality, Development and Peace"
1979 | CEDAW adopted (18 December)
1980 | Second World Conference, Copenhagen; CEDAW signing ceremony
1981 | CEDAW enters into force (3 September)
1984 | DAWN and WLUML founded
1985 | Third World Conference, Nairobi; Forward-Looking Strategies
1993 | Vienna World Conference on Human Rights; DEVAW (December)
1994 | ICPD, Cairo
1995 | Fourth World Conference, Beijing (4–15 September); Platform for Action; 189 governments; 12 critical areas
1996 | Pakistan accedes to CEDAW (12 March)
1999 | CEDAW Optional Protocol
2000 | Beijing+5; UNSCR 1325 (31 October); Millennium Development Goals
2010 | UN Women created (July; operational 2011; Michelle Bachelet)
2014 | HeForShe
2015 | SDG 5; Beijing+20
2020 / 2021 | Beijing+25; Generation Equality Forum
2025 | Beijing+30 (CSW, March)
:::

::: table | Beijing's 12 critical areas of concern.
@widths 1,13
@font 9.5
Area | Concern
A | Women and poverty
B | Education and training
C | Health
D | Violence against women
E | Armed conflict
F | The economy
G | Power and decision-making
H | Institutional mechanisms
I | Human rights of women
J | The media
K | The environment
L | The girl child
:::

## T3 · Pakistan: roots and the movement

::: table | The women's movement in Pakistan — timeline.
@widths 1.8,12.2
@font 9.5
Year | Event
1829 | Bengal Sati Regulation (Raja Ram Mohan Roy)
1856 | Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act (Vidyasagar)
1869 | Nazir Ahmad, *Mirat-ul-Uroos*
1891 | Age of Consent Act (10 → 12)
1898 | Mumtaz Ali, *Huquq-un-Niswan*; *Tahzib-e-Niswan* with Muhammadi Begum
1906 | Aligarh girls' school (Sheikh Abdullah and Wahid Jahan Begum)
1914 | All-India Muslim Ladies' Conference
1929 | Child Marriage Restraint Act (Sarda Act)
1937 | Shariat Application Act
1938 | Muslim League Central Women's Sub-Committee (Patna)
1939 | Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act
1944 | Quaid at Aligarh (10 March): "No nation can rise to the height of glory…"
1947 | Fatima Sughra's flag (February); Jahanara Shahnawaz and Shaista Ikramullah in the Constituent Assembly
1948 | West Punjab Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act
1949 | APWA (Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan)
1955–56 | Bogra's second marriage; Rashid Commission and report
1961 | Muslim Family Laws Ordinance
1965 | Fatima Jinnah contests the presidential election
1967 | *Khurshid Bibi* case — *khula* recognised
1973 | Constitution (Arts 25, 27, 32, 34, 35); Ra'ana Liaquat Governor of Sindh
1975 | Shirkat Gah
1979 | Hudood Ordinances (February); Women's Division; Tehrik-e-Niswan (theatre)
1980 | First all-women law firm (Asma Jahangir, Hina Jilani and colleagues)
1981 | WAF (Karachi) after the Fehmida–Allah Bux case
1982 | Sindhiani Tehreek
1983 | 12 February protest, Lahore; Safia Bibi case; ASR; Zari Sarfaraz Commission (to 1985)
1984 | Qanun-e-Shahadat Order; Ajoka theatre
1985–86 | Simorgh; Aurat Foundation
1987 | Mumtaz and Shaheed, *Women of Pakistan*; HRCP
1988 | Benazir Bhutto — first woman PM of a Muslim country
1989 | First Women Bank
1994 | Women's police stations; Lady Health Worker Programme; Al-Huda
1995 / 1996 | Beijing; CEDAW accession
1998 | National Plan of Action for Women
1999 | Samia Sarwar killed (honour killing)
2000 | NCSW; Okara tenants' movement begins
2001 | 33% women's seats in local government
2002 | 60 NA and 17 Senate reserved seats; National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women
2004 | Criminal Law (Amendment) Act (honour crimes)
2006 | Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act
2008 | Women's Parliamentary Caucus; Fahmida Mirza first woman Speaker; BISP
2010 | Workplace Harassment Act; 18th Amendment
2011 | Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act; Prevention of Anti-Women Practices Act
2012 | 12 February declared National Women's Day; NCSW Act; Supreme Court order on LHWs; *Saving Face* Oscar; Digital Rights Foundation
2016 | Qandeel Baloch killed (July); honour-killing and anti-rape amendments (October); PECA; Punjab PWVA; *A Girl in the River* Oscar
2017 | Elections Act (5% tickets; 10% women's turnout)
2018 | First Aurat March (8 March); WDF; Sindh Home-Based Workers Act; #MeToo reaches Pakistan
2020 | Motorway gang rape; Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act
2021 | Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act; Noor Mukadam murder
2024 | Maryam Nawaz — first woman chief minister
2025 | ICT Child Marriage Restraint Act (age 18); ICT Domestic Violence Act
:::

## T3 · Key comparisons

::: table | APWA and WAF.
@widths 3,5.5,5.5
@font 9.5
Point | APWA | WAF
Founded | 1949, Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan | 1981, Karachi
Trigger | Partition relief | Fehmida–Allah Bux stoning sentence
Aim | Welfare, education, health, skills | Resist discriminatory laws; women's rights
Method | Service; cooperation with the state | Protest, lobbying, legal analysis
Politics | Non-political, reformist | Secular, non-party, non-hierarchical
Funding | Charity and state support | Refused donor funding
:::

::: table | Suffragists and suffragettes.
@widths 3,5.5,5.5
@font 9.5
Point | Suffragists | Suffragettes
Organisation | NUWSS (1897) | WSPU (1903)
Leader | Millicent Fawcett | Emmeline and Christabel Pankhurst
Methods | Petitions, meetings, lobbying | Militancy — window-smashing, arson, hunger strikes
Slogan | — | "Deeds, not words"
:::

